Compare NSISdl socket errors against Winsock codes

NSISdl checks WSAGetLastError() against EINPROGRESS and EWOULDBLOCK,
which netinc.h defines as WSAEWOULDBLOCK only when they are not already
defined. With mingw-w64, windows.h reaches errno.h, which defines them
as the CRT's POSIX codes, 112 and 140, so the fallbacks never apply and
no comparison can match a Winsock error such as WSAEWOULDBLOCK (10035).
A non-blocking connect() reports WSAEWOULDBLOCK while it proceeds, so
every NSISdl::download failed at once with "connecting to host". Past
connect(), a recv() with no data yet likewise closed the connection, so
fixing connect() alone only turns that into "download incomplete".

Compare against WSAEWOULDBLOCK directly in all five places, and drop
the fallbacks so the CRT values cannot creep back in. EINPROGRESS maps
to WSAEWOULDBLOCK on purpose, as upstream intended: in Winsock,
WSAEINPROGRESS means a blocking call is already running, and connect()
does not report it for a non-blocking socket.

